i'm madison mills this is bloomberg  quick take the cost of food energy and  housing surged in november as americans  endure the highest annual inflation  since 1982. for u.s consumers prices  jumped nearly 7 in november from a year  ago as inflation intensifies pressure on  lower income households and especially  for those everyday necessities ahead of  the holiday season  so how will this affect wages and those  christmas gift lists and the big ticket  purchases that you may have been  planning to make all year  we want to welcome back marcia mantell  from mantel investments she is our  personal finance guru  marsha inflation has been a constant  theme this past year and the latest  numbers are certainly eye-popping but i  wonder how much of it is because we're  kind of less attentive to the true price  of goods there are some economists who  say that consumers are mainly shocked by  rising inflation because we had such a  long period of low inflation before the  pandemic so my question to you is do you  think americans have become too  comfortable with just expecting prices  to hold at current levels  i do i absolutely agree with that  comment and and it's easy to get lulled  into that  scarlett and maddie because it hasn't  been like it's been a year or two that  we've had this  managed inflation at around one and a  half to two and a half percent  it's been since the 80s since since i  graduated from high school and college  in 1983  we've had this very steady you know  little bumps here and there but  generally steady  slow increase in prices and our wages  have generally been able to keep up  but all of a sudden we're getting some  pretty snazzy headlines today that this  is you know very dramatic to have a 6.8  increase in all items from last november  to this november so that's quite  substantial it's not two percent  but it's also not as dramatic as it  seems on the surface it may not be as  dramatic for everyone but for me it  feels like the most  intense financial downfall of my  lifetime not to be you know overdramatic  or anything um you know i'm i'm my  family's from tampa they have the  highest inflation rate in the country  i'm gonna be there for the holidays in a  few weeks um what's next in terms of  when i can expect this to level off or  is this just how it's going to be from  now on  no you know maddie i i appreciate your  comments and as you know i have  girls your age as well and and it is a  little shocking you know things that you  bought  six months ago are now  are noticeably higher or for the same  hundred dollars you cannot fill that  grocery bag with as much stuff as you  used to  so this is a real thing especially when  food gets involved you know the big  indicators and the big inflation busters  right now are housing rent vehicles well  if you're not buying a new house if  you're not buying getting a new  apartment and seeing rent increases and  if you're not buying a new car that  wouldn't really matter  but food and gas  and fuel oil for the the folks of us who  those of us who live in the north  you feel it you see it in your  pocketbook you see that your dollar  doesn't stretch so far  but i want to give you a little  perspective because i mentioned in the  in the early 80s late 70s early 80s  there was a four year run of extreme  what i would call extreme inflation i'm  talking double digits every single month  the year-over-year numbers moved up 13  14 17. and it went on for four years  this doesn't feel the same for us older  timers uh it really doesn't that was a  particular era where we had all kinds of  situations that  that made for inflationary high  inflationary situations what we have  today is this real rub and i know you  guys talk about this all the time the  supply and demand you know our supply is  a lot less when our demand is a lot  higher  and that you know this is where all  boats rise  and in this case it's with inflation so  what to expect  you know there's still going to be some  some bouncing around of cost  inflation overall and pretty much the  only direction costs go are up  but what we'd like to see is a little  steadier you know  0.2 percent per month or a half a  percent per month  and and that's where i think two things  maddie one is that  there will still be  increases that you're going to feel real  consumers are really going to feel these  prices for a while yet but i encourage  you to look underneath the big headline  number  it isn't 6.8 for everything yeah and  you know i talk about ice cream you know  that's one of my favorite foods and  that's one of the items i track i am  happy to report ice cream has not gone  up nearly that high oh thank god  what you're gonna do is look for look  for good news within the harder to take  news i'm gonna adjust accordingly i'm  gonna eat less beef and go with ice  cream which i've always wanted to do  anyway but here's your reason exactly  marcia i mean there will be a lot of  finger pointing at the central bank and  at the white house and we know when we  had this period of rapid inflation in  the late 70s and early 80s the  prescription is to raise interest rates  and that's exactly what paul volcker did  at the federal reserve to the chagrin of  so many people because it was it was a  hard adjustment from where you sit is  that the most effective way policy  makers and elected officials can fight  inflation or is there another way to  slow down the price increase  that is a great question you know i  don't have that  great policy phd background in economics  so i'm going to let the economist speak  to that but what i will say is when  prices start to move higher we also need  to see things like wages increasing and  the interest rates for our savings  accounts to also increase so that we  don't see such a huge disparity between  high costs and purchasing power so  rising interest rates i listened to a  few articles this morning and read a few  things you know that's already on the  table  um  it's going to be interesting to watch  over the next few months and it's hard  when inflation has this high a jump at  the holiday time you know we're already  buying a lot more than maybe we normally  do i think we just all need to take a  little bit of a breath wait until  february and march let's see where the  supply gets bigger demand settles in  that's not holiday  and see where our numbers end up  absolutely and maddie that would be good  news for people who have been saving  money and don't collect a lot of  interest for it if interest rates do  start to increase that's true and that  was one of my big questions for you  marcia for the folks who uh are  getting closer to retirement i mean how  concerned should they be and is there  any way to kind of gain inflation to  your benefit  oh isn't that a good question  the best way to  kind of bend inflation and make sure  that it works for you is to make sure  you have a really good investment  strategy that has a significant  component for growth  so again all of these factors have to  balance each other out and the more you  know that or see that and make sure your  investments are aligned to we always  talk about it in the industry is you  want to have investments that beat  inflation or hedge inflation  well we're living it now it's no longer  a concept you know so we really need to  make sure that we don't get nervous and  you know sell  and and make big bold moves and pendulum  swing when that is not a strategy that's  a reaction so make sure your strategy is  really tight and in place if you have a  financial advisor talk to that financial  advisor make sure you understand what's  going on because frankly the market is  up it's gangbusters right but now we  have some higher inflation so it's  offsetting purchasing power and future  purchasing power so i i think we all  just need to get a little more connected  to how the markets move and when one of  the  the pistons you know moves a little  faster than it usually does yeah  absolutely marcia for a country of  savers uh inflation is bad news  because it devalues what you have there  unless of course you can make up for it  with rising investments but for a  country with a lot of debt rising  inflation is good because it also  devalues the debt  my question too is does that same kind  of thinking apply to individuals and  households like if you have a lot of  consumer debt if you have a lot of  credit card debt or student debt is  inflation good news for you  well i think overall when you have  consumer debt not mortgage mortgage is  kind of its own category  it is very tough if you're not keeping  up with your credit card payments i mean  the interest rates already are so very  high  and where  where i look at it from that consumer  you know literally you know mom and pop  sitting down to pay bills  when inflation is rising and they have  to pay more at the grocery store they  have less less to pay that credit card  outstanding balance and because the  rates on credit cards for example are so  high  you end up you know having to elongate  and stretch out your payments therefore  paying more  so  it's more important to have a good debt  approach you know if you can don't get  yourself over your skis here with debt  but if you have really work hard to chip  away at that debt high interest rates  not so good having to spend money now at  the grocery store instead of paying down  debt not so good yeah  really quickly in our final 30 seconds  here what's your number one biggest tip  for dealing with inflation  budget  get reacquainted with your budget ladies  it is really looking hard at where  you're spending and where can you make  some trade-offs as we go through this  bumpy juncture here right right if  anything maybe i can convince myself  it's an opportunity to get reacquainted  with budgeting again marcia always  appreciate speaking with you thank you  so much for joining us today that was  marcia mantel from mantel investments

